[[WEB_SEARCHES:final 2026 Senate forecast October 31 2026 Republican majority odds seats||2026 Senate polls final week Maine North Carolina Ohio Alaska Texas Nebraska Iowa October 2026||Sabato Cook 2026 Senate ratings final October 2026]]

Mr. Chair, the evidence in the final week points to a Senate battlefield that has tilted toward the Democrats, though not so far that I would call a Republican collapse certain. The starting point is 53 Republicans and 47 Democrats, with 35 seats on the ballot and 23 of those Republican-held, as 270toWin lays out at https://www.270towin.com/2026-senate-election-predictions. The race-by-race signals are not ambiguous. NewsNation reported on October 26 that Decision Desk HQ gave Democrats a 57 percent chance of winning the Senate majority, and DDHQ’s forecast page at https://votes.decisiondeskhq.com/forecast/2026/senate shows Democratic-leaning ratings in North Carolina at 85 percent, Georgia at 86 percent, New Hampshire at 84 percent, and Minnesota at 79 percent, while Florida and Louisiana remain likely Republican. RealClearPolling’s latest Senate page at https://www.realclearpolling.com/latest-polls/senate shows Maine’s Collins-Jackson contest tied at 47, and NBC’s battleground polling has Talarico ahead of Paxton in Texas, Ossoff ahead in Georgia, and Cooper with a large lead in North Carolina. Cook’s October 29 closing analysis frames seven states as decisive, and Polymarket’s balance-of-power market puts “Democrats Sweep” at 72 percent, though that is a broader midterm outcome rather than a pure Senate reading. The caveat is that the Senate map still requires Democrats to convert several Republican-held seats, and the Cook and 270toWin consensus tables still show the final composition close to the break-even line. So I would not call it a lock, but I would call the Republican majority a clear underdog. My estimate is that Republicans have roughly a one-in-four to one-in-three chance of holding the Senate, which means I would put the probability of a Republican majority at about 30 percent. If the question is specifically Democratic majority, the complement is about 70 percent. I would express the Republican-hold probability as a 20 to 40 percent range, centered near 30.

{"probability": {"low": 20, "high": 40}}

